{"kind":"artifact","text":"Exact-head implementation artifact at path internal/kernel for the race-aware allocation-count repair, exact pushed head a0e9454922b2934e5494eb11dde9cca8ce8b2d5f on branch request/kernel-race-aware-alloc-count, cut from current main 5ff5d4db648874d5619db33c67f0b1c801b5af0b. This is the implementation report for planner request #10746 under my promise #10749.\n\nTHE CHANGE. Three files, +24/-4, no non-test code touched. Two new build-tag files define a constant: race_on_test.go (//go:build race) sets raceEnabled = true, race_off_test.go (//go:build !race) sets it false. Three allocation ceilings in internal/kernel/checkpoint_cache_test.go are then checked only when the detector is absent — `if allocs \u003e N` becomes `if !raceEnabled \u0026\u0026 allocs \u003e N` at lines 541, 825 and 859. One comment sentence was added to the scratch-preflight doc block recording why the ceiling is race-conditional. internal/kernel/checkpoint.go is unmodified.\n\nWHY. testing.AllocsPerRun measures allocations per call; the race detector adds its own per-call bookkeeping, so under -race the number describes the instrumentation rather than the refusal path. The measurement was not wrong, it was being taken where it cannot mean what it says.\n\nSCOPE WIDENED, DELIBERATELY. The request named one test. Three had the same latent defect and all three are fixed here, because repairing only the failing one leaves the gate red on the next -race run that reaches another:\n- TestCheckpointCacheScratchPreflightRefusesBeforeAllocation (checkpoint_cache_test.go:816) — the one that was RED. Non-race 8, ceiling 8; under -race it measured 9 in a full-package run.\n- TestCheckpointCacheRefusesOversizedEventBeforeCloning (:534) — passing but latent: non-race 4, race 5, ceiling 8.\n- TestCompactCheckpointWriterRefusesAboveDecoderCeiling (:852) — passing with ZERO headroom: non-race 1, race 1, ceiling 1. The next instrumentation change would have turned this red.\nNo twins elsewhere. AllocsPerRun appears repo-wide only in that one file; b.ReportAllocs() in internal/app, internal/statusview, internal/workroom and internal/kernel benchmarks only reports and asserts nothing; internal/perfscenario/run.go reads MemStats as a perf harness, not a gate. Nothing to follow up in other packages.\n\nCONDITIONS MET, with gates I ran myself at this exact head rather than relayed:\n- go test -count=1 ./internal/kernel/ — ok, 219.728s.\n- go test -race -count=1 ./internal/kernel/ — ok, 156.613s.\n- go vet ./internal/kernel/ — clean, exit 0.\n- The alloc ceiling still bites without -race, and I proved it rather than asserting it. Tightening the scratch ceiling from 8 to 7 and running that test alone: non-race FAILS with \"preflight refusal allocated 8 times\", -race passes. That demonstrates both halves of the guard in one probe — the assertion is live without the detector and correctly skipped under it — and incidentally confirms the true non-race count is exactly 8.\n- No functional coverage removed. The refusal-behaviour assertions inside each measured closure are left unguarded and run under both modes. Proved: with c.fail(err) removed from the error path in appendBorrowedChunk, and the earlier assertCacheReleased block temporarily lifted so the closure is reached, both runs fail with \"scratch past the budget was admitted: \u003cnil\u003e\" — the line-819 assertion inside AllocsPerRun.\n- Mutation that allocates in the refusal path: adding a names-slice allocation before the scratch check fails the no-race run with 9 allocations and passes under -race, exactly as intended.\n- Mutation that breaks refusal behaviour: removing c.fail(err) fails BOTH runs with \"cache error = \u003cnil\u003e, want checkpoint exceeds byte limit\".\nAll mutations were reverted before the commit, and the gate runs above are on the clean committed tree.\n\nONE HONEST NEGATIVE, recorded because a reviewer should have it. The first attempt at the break-the-refusal mutation did NOT fail as expected, and the reason is a real property of the test rather than of this change. Replacing the scratch preflight condition with `if false` left refused.err still errCheckpointTooLarge: the bounded chunk writer refuses that input downstream anyway, just later and after the names slice is allocated. So disabling the preflight moves WHERE the refusal happens, not WHETHER it happens, and only the allocation ceiling catches it — which is precisely the ordering property the test is named for, but means the test does not pin the preflight's existence through its behaviour assertion alone. That is pre-existing and not introduced here; I am not proposing to change it under this request. If the reviewer judges it worth pinning directly, it is a child request, not a change to this head.\n\nKNOWN TIGHTNESS, not a defect: two of the three ceilings sit at their measured non-race counts with zero headroom (8 of 8, and 1 of 1). That is intentional in the original tests — the comments say the ceiling is the measured count so even one extra allocation fails — and this change preserves it exactly.\n\nSUCCESSION NOTE for step five: I have not checked which live artifacts at internal/kernel this head would retire, because retirement is merge-sealed and belongs at merge time, not to this artifact.","body":{"branch":"request/kernel-race-aware-alloc-count","commit":"a0e9454922b2934e5494eb11dde9cca8ce8b2d5f","conditions":"go test -race -count=1 ./internal/kernel/ and go test -count=1 ./internal/kernel/ both green at this exact head; go vet clean; the alloc-count ceilings still enforced without -race and proved live by tightening one ceiling (non-race fails, race passes); no functional test coverage removed, proved by a refusal-behaviour mutation failing both runs; three latent instances fixed, no twins elsewhere in the repo.","head":"a0e9454922b2934e5494eb11dde9cca8ce8b2d5f","path":"internal/kernel"}}